  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM EST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\n- Light rain showers persist today, shift to the lakeshore Friday.\n\nBehind yesterday afternoon's cold front, the combination of cooler \ntemperatures aloft (850mb temps of 2-4C), warm lake temperatures (14-\n16C), and ambient low level moisture upstream continues to produce \nlight but widespread lake-enhanced showers across lower Michigan. \nThe greatest coverage early this morning has been north of a South \nHaven to Lansing line due to persistent southwesterly low level \nflow, with peak strength roughly along the US-131 corridor. While \nthese showers will fluctuate through the morning and afternoon due \nto subtle temperature/wind fluctuations, these showers should \nlargely persist. Meanwhile, expect much more typical temperatures \nfor early November, with highs expected to top out in the low to mid \n50s...very near seasonal averages. \n\nLater this evening, a reinforcing cold front will push through the \narea as a remnant surface low slowly moves southeast across Lake \nHuron. Behind this front, cold air advection will bring 850 mb \ntemperatures down to around -2 to -4C, resulting in a nearly 15-19C \ndifference from Lake Michigan water temperatures. However, winds \nwill abruptly shift to the north-northwest behind the front as \ncyclonic flow develops, and eventually to almost due northerly. So, \nwhile lake-enhanced (and eventually purely lake effect) rain showers \nmay intensify, most of these are expected to shift out to the open \nwaters and along the immediate lakeshore by Friday morning. \nMeanwhile, ambient light rain that is not driven by lake effects are \nexpected to gradually diminish through the night and Friday morning, \nso many interior areas should see mostly dry conditions during the \nday Friday. This may take longer to materialize in our far northeast \n(roughly M-20 and north) as these areas will be closer to the \nsurface low, and may also see remnant lake effect bands from the \nnorthern Lakes. It also wouldn't be a shock to see a few wet (and \nnon-accumulating) snowflakes in the higher terrain of our far north \nearly Friday morning, but this should be short-lived if it \nmaterializes. \n\nOtherwise, expect temperatures to dip into the 30s by early Friday \nmorning, with perhaps a brief, light freeze in our far north. Friday \nafternoon will likewise be much cooler, with highs only topping out \nin the mid 40s to near 50 degrees, just a smidge below the daily \naverage but on par with a typical early November day.\n\n- Cooler, more seasonable temperatures continue this weekend onward.\n\nLake effect showers should diminish by Friday night, and from here \nout the Great Lakes will enter a generally dry northwest flow \npattern, with a more active southern stream across the southern \nCONUS. A weak \"clipper\" type shortwave will pass the area to the \nnortheast Sunday, which should reinforce seasonably cool air but is \nnot expected to produce meaningful precipitation chances, lake-\neffect or otherwise. Instead, we will see dry conditions through the \nweekend, with morning lows near or slightly below freezing afternoon \nhighs in the 50s. Longer range guidance also suggests that shortwave \nridging may drive a very modest warming trend early next week, but \nfor the most part temperatures are likely to remain near or just \nabove average, with minimal precipitation chances.",
